  "query_preview": "..\nThursday and Friday: An upper level trough remains positioned over \nthe Inland Northwest through this period with embedded shortwaves \nrippling through the broader flow pattern. Temperatures on Thursday \nwill remain around 3 to 7 degrees cooler than early July averages. \nHeading into Friday, highs warm into the upper 70s to mid 80s. \nSurface heating combined with passing shortwaves will create enough \ninstability to bring a 10-15 percent chance of showers and \nthunderstorms over the Cascade crest Thursday evening and along the \nCanadian border Friday. Cool marine air over Western WA and warmer \nair over Eastern WA will bring a recurring push of westerly winds \nthrough the Cascade gaps each evening through the end of the \nworkweek.\n\nSaturday and Sunday: The region shifts into more of a zonal flow \npattern as a flat ridge takes the place of the upper level trough \nover the holiday weekend. This shift will drive a more pronounced \nwarming and drying trend across the region with high temperatures \nclimbing well into the 80s Saturday. In addition to warmer \ntemperatures, a dry frontal passage will bring gusty winds and \nhumidities falling into the teens to low 20s regionwide. Expect west-\nsouthwest winds Saturday afternoon with sustained speeds of 10-25 \nmph and gusts of 20-40 mph. The warm, dry, and breezy conditions \nwill bring elevated to critical fire weather conditions for the \nIndependence Day holiday. Warm and dry conditions continue Sunday. \nBreezy north winds will linger down the Okanogan Valley into Sunday \nmorning, but for the rest of the region, Sunday's winds will be \nlighter.\n\nMonday through Wednesday: The warming trend continues into next week \nwith a ridge amplifying over the Northern Rockies, sending high \ntemperatures into the 90s across the Inland Northwest. There is high \nconfidence that Monday and Tuesday will be hot, but model solutions \ndiverge toward the middle of the week with how long the ridging \npattern (and heat) will last. /Fewkes",
